      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3fd03b4b-26b0-57a3-90b6-cf64c012470d",
        ":question/problem": "Find the **positive** $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=- x^{3} + 42 x - 2$ whose tangent has a slope of $-6.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- x^{3} + 42 x - 2.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f^{\\prime}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime}(x) &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- x^{3} + 42 x - 2\\right) \\\\\n&= (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot x^{2} + (42)\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 42 - 3 x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f^{\\prime}(x)=-6$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n42 - 3 x^{2} &= -6 \\\\\n48 - 3 x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n- 3 \\left(x - 4\\right) \\left(x + 4\\right) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -4,\\,4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$, the required coordinate is $x=4.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
